That is her ransom; I deliver her;
And those two counties I will undertake
Your grace shall well and quietly enjoy.

Ythrild with deepe disdaine of his proud threat,
She shortly thus; Fly they, that need to fly;
Wordes fearen babes.

[…] Admiration seized
All Heaven, what this might mean, and whither tend,
Wondering;

A little bird sat on the edge of her nest;
Her yellow-beaks slept as sound as tops;
That day she had done her very best,
And had filled every one of their little crops.

Tell age it daily wasteth;
tell honour how it alters;
Tell beauty how she blasteth;
tell fauour how it falters:
And as they shall reply,
giue euery one the lye.

[…] to the lute
She sung, and made the night-bird mute,
That still records with moan;

Slow tolls the village-clock the drowſy hour;
The partridge burſts away on whirring wings;
Deep mourns the turtle in ſequeſter'd bower,
And ſhrill lark carols clear from her aereal tour.

When I do count the clock that tells the time,
And see the brave day sunk in hideous night;
When I behold the violet past prime,
And sable curls all silver’d o'er with white;

Flying bullets now,
 To execute his rage, appear too slow;
 They miss, or sweep but common souls away.

Thus conscience does make cowards of us all;
And thus the native hue of resolution
Is sicklied o'er with the pale cast of thought.
